.TH man_3_printf 3 "Feb 02, 2025" "Custom Library" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
_printf - A custom implementation of the printf function.

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "main.h"

int _printf(const char *format, ...);
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fB_printf\fR function prints formatted output to the standard output (stdout), similar to the standard \fBprintf(3)\fR function.
It processes a format string and a variable number of arguments, supporting a subset of standard format specifiers.

Supported format specifiers:
.TP
\fB%c\fR
Prints a single character.
.TP
\fB%s\fR
Prints a string.
.TP
\fB%d, %i\fR
Prints a signed integer in decimal format.
.TP
\fB%u\fR
Prints an unsigned integer.
.TP
\fB%o\fR
Prints an unsigned integer in octal format.
.TP
\fB%x, %X\fR
Prints an unsigned integer in hexadecimal format (lowercase or uppercase).
.TP
\fB%%\fR
Prints a percent sign.

.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the number of characters printed (excluding the null byte used to end output to strings).
Returns -1 if an error occurs.

.SH ERRORS
.TP
-1
Returned when the format string is null or contains an invalid format specifier.

.SH EXAMPLES
Example usage of \fB_printf\fR:
.nf
#include "main.h"

int main() {
int count = _printf("Hello, %s! The number is %d\n", "world", 42);
_printf("Printed %d characters.\n", count);
return 0;
}
.fi

